            <ArticleTitle>Internal resorption occurring after accidental extrusion of iodoform paste into the mandibular canal.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>81-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The precise etiology of internal resorption is unknown but it is generally believed that triggering factors are infection and persistent chronic pulpitis. A case is presented with internal resorption, which developed subsequent to extrusion of iodoform paste from the adjacent tooth into the mandibular canal. According to clinical and radiographic examinations made after 7 years, the tooth was clinically asymptomatic, vital and the internal resorption was arrested without treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
